  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nUpstream radar and satellite observations show an area of low\npressure over central Indiana tracking slowly eastward, pushing\nan arcing line of showers and thunderstorms through western\nKentucky and Ohio toward the forecast area. Some transient\nrotation has been noted within the line. As this activity moves\ninto northeast Kentucky, southeast Ohio, and the Metro Valley \nthis evening, the loss of daytime surface heating will lead to a\nweakening trend in storm intensity. MLCAPE values, which peaked\nnear 1000J/kg earlier today, are expected to fall below 200J/kg\novernight.\n\nDespite diminishing thermodynamic instability, atmospheric\nmoisture remains unseasonably high across the region with\nPrecipitable water values ranging between 1.75 and 2.00 inches.\nSteering winds through H500 from the south-southwest at 20 to \n25KTs remain nearly parallel to the incoming frontal boundary. \nThis alignment will support limited south-southwest to north- \nnortheast training of heavy rain cells before activity tapers \noff late tonight, maintaining a localized risk for minor high \nwater in low-lying areas.\n\nA more favorable environment for convective organization\ndevelops on Sunday as the mid-level closed low drifts into the\nOhio Valley and synoptic forcing increases. Differential daytime\nheating through breaks in cloud cover will allow MLCAPE to\nrebuild to 800 to 1200J/kg by Sunday afternoon. A low-level jet\nat H850 with winds of 30 to 35KTs will shift near or just east \nof the eastern border during peak heating. Threat for a \nbrief tornado remains low and primarily focused along the \neastern belt of stronger low-level flow. \n\nThe primary convective hazard on Sunday afternoon and evening\nwill be localized damaging wind gusts with modest low-level \nlapse rates exceeding 6.5C/km will support isolated downbursts\nespecially in any breaks to full sunshine.\n\nThe flash flood risk will also be elevated on Sunday afternoon\nand evening. Deep warm cloud depths exceeding 11k feet will \nfavor efficient warm rain processes within the moisture-rich \nairmass. Steering flow aligned parallel to the surface boundary \nwill favor training convective cells from south-southwest to \nnorth-northeast. The highest risk for training heavy rain and \nflash flooding is oriented along the I-79 and US-119 corridors \neastward into the Northeast Mountains, where steep terrain and \nnarrow hollows will rapidly direct runoff into small streams. A \nFlash Flood Watch may ultimately be required for these eastern \nareas for Sunday afternoon and evening depending on model\ntrends.\n\nAs of 1053 AM Saturday...\n\nAn area of low pressure is currently seen on GOES satellite over \ncentral Illinois, moving eastward. Showers are seen tracking \neastward ahead of low pressure from Tennessee, Kentucky, and \nsouthwestern Ohio. Showers and a few thunderstorms will impact \nmainly northeast Kentucky, western West Virginia, and southeast Ohio \ntoday and tonight.\n\nAs low pressure slowly tracks east into Kentucky Sunday, \nareawide showers and thunderstorms are expected across the\nforecast area. If enough destabilization occurs during the day\nwith enough breaks in the clouds, a few strong thunderstorms \ncould develop. Some models indicate that this will occur with \n800-1,000 J/kg of MLCAPE developing by Sunday afternoon. The \nmain threat with any thunderstorms would be localized damaging \nwind gusts. SPC currently has a marginal risk of severe weather \noutlooked across the forecast area. \n\nFlooding will also be a concern with rounds of showers and \nstorms tracking southwest to northeast throughout the day. If \ntraining cells impact the same areas with repeated rounds of \nheavy rain, flooding could occur. The WPC currently has a \nmarginal risk of excessive rainfall across the forecast area for\nSunday. Rainfall amounts throughout the weekend are expected to\nbe about a half inch to an inch, with localized amounts up to\none and a half inches possible. The heaviest amounts will be\nacross the western half of the forecast area.\n\nThe threat of showers and thunderstorms will continue into the start \nof the new work week. Models indicate that the same low pressure \nsystem impacting the forecast area this weekend will be slow to \nexit, and an upper-level cutoff low may develop either Monday or \nTuesday somewhere in the Ohio Valley or Southeast. This will allow \nfor the continued development of showers and thunderstorms, \nespecially each afternoon and early evening during peak \nheating.",
